  "notes": "Port Salerno taunting street trap: loud, on-beat, chant-first. The two parameters that make it are absence and repetition — a sparse two-note bell ostinato over a static tonic, with the claps doing the shouting. `melody.motifPitchCount` is the honest reading of \"sparse two-note bell/pluck ostinati\", and it is set as a range rather than a fixed 2 so the pitch set can breathe without becoming a tune. ⛔ `chords.impliedByRiff` is set: the research says the harmony is a static tonic and the occasional soul flip, so the ostinato *is* the changes. It is a description rather than an exemption — the chord part still owes 55 distinct outputs over 200 seeds, and what carries that here is `chordDurationBeats` [2,14] plus three `harmonicRhythm` values plus a wide voicing register, not progression families the records do not play. ⚠ `confidence` is medium and the reason is specific: the research marks the drum and 808 rows [LOW-CONF]. The kick's `syncopation` 0.52 / `tresilloBias` 0.24 and the whole `bass808` block are INFERRED from \"punchy syncopated kick\" and \"punchy short-to-mid 808 with fast 16th repeats\" — they are a reading of two prose lines, not a producer's account. The era, tempo band and chant-hook approach are the parts the chart interviews actually confirm. ⚠ The research says 32nd hat rolls; this entry's roll vocabulary is `16T` because that is this author's fill band, and the triplet reading is the one that survives — the gesture is a fast triplet burst into bar 4 either way. ⚠ `anticipationProb` raised to 0.15. With `mirror_kick` against a fully locked 808 both parts take every kick, so every bass onset lands exactly on an 808 onset — `coherence.rs` calls that one line played twice rather than two parts locking. The lock is the style and is untouched; the anticipation is what makes them two parts.",
